\\).  The rectangle must contain all columns between \\\\(c_i\\\\) and \\\\(c_{i'}\\\\); in particular it contains column \\\\(c_i+1\\\\) in row \\\\(i\\\\).  Since the row‑lengths are distinct we have \\\\(c_i<c_{i'}\\\\), and the cell \\\\((i,c_i+1)\\\\) does not belong to \\\\(R^+\\\\) (it lies to the left of the left‑corner of row \\\\(i\\\\)).  This contradicts the assumption that the rectangle stays inside \\\\(R^+\\\\).  Hence each rectangle covers at most one left‑corner.\\nSince there are \\\\(n-1\\\\) left‑corners in \\\\(R^+\\\\), any tiling of \\\\(R^+\\\\) requires at least \\\\(n-1\\\\) rectangles.\\nThe same argument applied to \\\\(R^-\\\\) (using the right‑corners of each non‑empty row) shows that at least \\\\(n-1\\\\) rectangles are needed for \\\\(R^-\\\\) as well.\\nTherefore any admissible placement of tiles uses at least\\n\\\\[2(n-1)\\\\]\\ntiles.\\n\\n**3. A construction attaining the bound.**\\nWe now exhibit a placement with exactly \\\\(2(n-1)\\\\) tiles.\\nChoose the cyclic permutation\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\pi(i)=\\\\begin{cases}\\n i+1 & \\\\text{if }1\\\\le i<n,\\\\\\\\\\n 1   & \\\\text{if }i=n.\\n\\\\end{cases}\\n\\\\]\\nThus the uncovered squares are \\\\((1,2),(2,3),\\\\dots,(n-1,n),(n,1)\\\\).\\n\\n*Covering \\\\(R^+\\\\).*  For each column \\\\(c\\\\) with \\\\(2\\\\le c\\\\le n\\\\) consider the set\\n\\\\[\\\\mathcal R_c=\\\\{i\\\\mid \\\\pi(i)<c\\\\}.\\\\]\\nBecause \\\\(\\\\pi(i)<c\\\\) exactly when the uncovered square of row \\\\(i\\\\) lies strictly left of column \\\\(c\\\\), the cells of \\\\(R^+\\\\) that lie in column \\\\(c\\\\) are precisely the squares \\\\((i,c)\\\\) with \\\\(i\\\\in\\\\mathcal R_c\\\\).  Place a vertical tile covering exactly those squares; its dimensions are \\\\(|\\\\mathcal R_c|\\\\times1\\\\).  The tiles for different values of \\\\(c\\\\) are disjoint, and together they cover every cell of \\\\(R^+\\\\) (every cell of \\\\(R^+\\\\) belongs to a unique column \\\\(c\\\\)).  This uses \\\\(n-1\\\\) tiles.\\n\\n*Covering \\\\(R^-\\\\).*  By symmetry we treat the left side.  For each row \\\\(r\\\\) with \\\\(2\\\\le r\\\\le n\\\\) let\\n\\\\[\\\\mathcal C_r=\\\\{j\\\\mid \\\\pi^{-1}(j)>r\\\\}.\\\\]\\nThe condition \\\\(\\\\pi^{-1}(j)>r\\\\) means that the uncovered square in column \\\\(j\\\\) lies strictly below row \\\\(r\\\\).  Consequently the cells of \\\\(R^-\\\\) that lie in row \\\\(r\\\\) are exactly \\\\((r,j)\\\\) with \\\\(j\\\\in\\\\mathcal C_r\\\\).  Place a horizontal tile covering precisely those squares; its dimensions are \\\\(1\\\\times|\\\\mathcal C_r|\\\\).  As \\\\(r\\\\) varies, the tiles are disjoint and together they cover all of \\\\(R^-\\\\).  Again we obtain \\\\(n-1\\\\) tiles.\\n\\nThe uncovered squares are precisely the permutation squares \\\\((i,\\\\pi(i))\\\\), one per row and column, as required.  The total number of tiles is\\n\\\\[ (n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)=4048.\\\\]\\n\\n**4. Minimality.**\\nThe lower bound of Section 2 shows that no placement can use fewer than \\\\(2(n-1)\\\\) tiles, while the construction of Section 3 achieves exactly that number.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n\\\\[\\\\boxed{4048}.\\\\]\\n\\n**5. Conclusion.**\\nFor the $2025\\\\times2025$ grid the smallest number of rectangular tiles that can be placed so that each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square is $4048$.\\n\\n---\\n\\n*All arguments are elementary and avoid the previously forbidden diagonal‑permutation and Ferrers‑diagram constructions; the lower bound relies on a corner‑counting argument, and the upper bound uses a cyclic permutation together with a column‑/row‑threshold tiling.*\",\n  \"method\": \"summary\",\n  \"final_answer\": 4040\n}"
