ring (i_j, j) also cover cells in other rows that also have rightmost column j? Possibly, but the argument says each column j is the rightmost column of a distinct tile covering L. Could a tile covering (i_j, j) also cover (i_{j'}, j) for some other row i_{j'}? That would be a tile that spans multiple rows, but its rightmost column is j. However, the tile would also contain the uncovered squares in those other rows? Let's examine: For any other row i_{j'}, the uncovered square is at column π(i_{j'}). For column j to be the rightmost column of a tile covering (i_{j'}, j), we need that column j < π(i_{j'}), otherwise (i_{j'}, j) would be not left of the uncovered square. But since we have defined i_{j'} such that π(i_{j'}) = j'+1, we have π(i_{j'}) = j'+1. So for j ≠ j', we have j < j'+1? Not necessarily. If j > j', then j could be ≥ j'+1, meaning that (i_{j'}, j) might be to the right of the uncovered square for row i_{j'}. But L only includes cells left of holes, i.e., j < π(i). So (i_{j'}, j) is in L only if j < π(i_{j'}) = j'+1. So for j ≥ j'+1, (i_{j'}, j) is not in L. So each column j appears as rightmost column for exactly one row's left-of-hole cell: the row where the uncovered square is at column j+1. So each column j is the rightmost column of exactly one tile covering L. However, could a tile cover multiple rows' left-of-hole cells while still having rightmost column j? For a tile to cover cells in multiple rows, it must be a rectangle covering a set of rows and columns. If its rightmost column is j, and it covers rows i_j and i_{j'} (with j' < j?), then for row i_{j'}, the cell (i_{j'}, j) might be left of the uncovered square if j < π(i_{j'}). But π(i_{j'}) = j'+1. So j < j'+1 implies j ≤ j'. So if j ≤ j', then j < j'+1 holds. So for j ≤ j', the cell (i_{j'}, j) is left of the uncovered square for row i_{j'}. So it's possible that a tile covering column j and rows i_j and i_{j'} could contain both (i_j, j) and (i_{j'}, j). But the tile's rightmost column 