 a single tile be the rightmost column for multiple columns? No. So we need at least n-1 tiles for columns 1..n-1. But could a tile be the rightmost column for column j and also leftmost column for column j+1? Possibly, but the argument for L and R separate. For L, we need tiles covering left side; for R, we need tiles covering right side. Could a tile cover both left side of some holes and right side of other holes? That would mean it includes cells left of some holes and right of others. But the tile's column interval is contiguous; if it includes both left and right of a hole, it would also include the hole itself (since the hole is between left and right). So a tile cannot cross a hole. So each tile must be entirely left of some holes or entirely right of some holes. However, a tile could be left of some holes and right of others if the holes are in different rows and the tile's column interval is not crossing any hole in those rows. Let's think: Suppose we have holes at positions (i, π(i)). A tile covers a set of rows and columns. For each row that tile covers, the tile must be either entirely left of the hole in that row or entirely right of it. If the tile covers multiple rows, the column interval must be consistent across rows. So if tile's column interval is [a,b], then for each row i covered, either b < π(i) (tile left of hole) or a > π(i) (tile right of hole). So the tile must be either left of all holes in rows it covers, or right of all holes in those rows. It cannot be left of some holes and right of others because that would require the interval to be both left of some π(i) and right of others, which could be possible if the intervals are disjoint? Let's examine: Suppose we have rows i1 and i2 with holes at columns π(i1)=2 and π(i2)=5. A tile covering rows i1 and i2 with columns [3,4] would be right of hole in row i1 (since 3 > 2) and left of hole in row i2 (since 4 < 5). This tile would be to the right of hole in row i1 and left of hole in row i2. But is that allowed? The tile would be crossing the hole in row i2? Actually, for row i2, the tile's columns [3,4] are left of hole at column 5, so okay. For row i1, tile's columns [3,4] are right of hole at column 2, okay. So the tile is to the right of hole in row i1 and left of hole in row i2. So the tile is not entirely left or right of all holes; it's mixed. Does that violate any condition? The condition is each row and column has exactly one uncovered square. The tile can be placed anywhere as long as it doesn't cover any uncovered squares. So it's allowed to be right of some holes and left of others. So the lower bound argument that each column j must be the rightmost column of a distinct tile covering L may be flawed because a tile could have rightmost column j but also cover cells left of holes in rows where the hole is to the right of column j, but also cover cells right of holes in rows where the hole is to the left of column j. However, the argument for L considered only cells left of holes. It considered each column j and the cell (i_j, j) which is the rightmost cell left of the hole in row i_j. The tile covering that cell cannot extend to column j+1 because that would cover the hole. So its rightmost column is j. But could the same tile also cover other cells left of holes in other rows? Yes, but those cells would be in column j as well (since rightmost column j). So the tile could cover multiple rows' cells left of holes, all in column j. That's fine. So each column j may be the rightmost column of a tile that covers many rows' left-of-hole cells. So we need at least n-1 tiles for L? Actually, we need at least one tile per column j (1..n-1). Since each column j must be the rightmost column of some tile covering L. But a single tile can be the rightmost column for multiple columns? No, because a tile has a single rightmost column. So each column j requires a distinct tile. So at least n-1 tiles for L. Similarly for R. So total at least 2n-2.\n\nBut is there any way to reduce total number of tiles by using some tiles that cover both L and R? That would require a tile that includes cells both left of some holes and right of other holes, but then the tile would have both leftmost column > some holes and rightmost column < other holes. That would mean the tile's column interval is between the holes of those rows. For a tile to cover both L and R, it would need to have some rows where the tile is left of the hole, and other rows where it's right of the hole. This is possible as earlier example. However, the lower bound argument counts tiles covering L and tiles covering R separately, but a tile could be counted in both categories? The argument defines L as all cells left of holes, and R as all cells right of holes. A tile covering both left and right cells would intersect both L and R. In the lower bound argument, they consider tiles covering L and tiles covering R separately, but they didn't argue that a tile cannot cover both L and R. They assumed each tile is either covering L or covering R exclusively. Actually they said: \"Consider any rectangular tile T that covers (i_j, j). Since the uncovered square in row i_j is at column j+1, the tile cannot extend to column j+1; otherwise it would also contain the uncovered square, which is forbidden. Because a tile’s column interval is contiguous, the rightmost column of T must be exactly j. Consequently each column j (1≤j≤n-1) is the rightmost column of a distinct tile covering L.\" That tile could also cover some cells right of holes in other rows (i.e., belong to R). But does that matter? The tile's rightmost column is j, so it cannot cover any cell with column > j. So it cannot cover any cell in R that lies to the right of a hole in some row if that cell's column > j. But R includes cells with column > π(i). For a row where π(i) < j, the cells right of hole are columns > π(i). Some of those columns could be ≤ j? Actually if π(i) < j, then right-of-hole cells are columns > π(i). Some of those columns could be ≤ j, but they are still left of column j. But the tile's rightmost column is j, so it could cover some right-of-hole cells that are ≤ j. But those cells are not in R? Wait, R is defined as cells (i,j) where π(i) < j ≤ n. So if j ≤ rightmost column of tile (which is j), then those cells are in column ≤ j. If π(i) < j, then (i,j) is in R. So tile could cover some cells in R that are in column j (the rightmost column). Similarly, a tile covering L may also cover some cells in R at column j. But the lower bound argument counts each column j as a distinct tile covering L; but that tile may also be counted as covering R for column j+1? Actually for R, they consider columns j from 2 to n, each column j is leftmost column of a distinct tile covering R. That tile's leftmost column is j. So a tile that has rightmost column j (covering L) cannot have leftmost column j (unless tile width zero). So the tile covering L for column j cannot be the same as tile covering R for column j (since leftmost column = rightmost column). So each tile counted for L is distinct from each tile counted for R. However, could a tile be counted for L for column j and also counted for R for some other column k? Possibly, but the tile's rightmost column is j, leftmost column is something else. For it to be leftmost column for column k (i.e., leftmost column = k), we would need k = leftmost column of that tile. Since leftmost column ≤ rightmost column, we could have k ≤ j. But the tile's leftmost column is some column less than j. Could it be that tile's leftmost column is k (≥2) and tile's rightmost column is j (≤ n-1). Then that tile would be counted for R for column k (since leftmost column = k) and for L for column j (rightmost column = j). But the lower bound argument for R says each column j (2 ≤ j ≤ n) is the leftmost column of a distinct tile covering R. So each column j must have a tile whose leftmost column is j. So if a tile's leftmost column is k, then it counts for column k in R. So a tile could be counted for both L and R if its leftmost column is some k (≥2) and its rightmost column is some j (≤ n-1). That would mean the tile covers columns from k to j. But then for column k, the tile is leftmost column, for column j, tile is rightmost column. So it would be counted twice, reducing total count? However, the argument for L says each column j (1..n-1) must be the rightmost column of a distinct tile covering L. Distinct tile meaning distinct for each column j, but could the same tile be the rightmost column for multiple columns? No. So each column j has a distinct tile. Similarly each column j (2..n) has a distinct tile for leftmost column. So we have at least (n-1)+(n-1) distinct tiles, but could some tile be counted for both a rightmost column j and a leftmost column k? That would mean the same tile is counted for two columns, but the argument says \"distinct tile covering L\" for each j, and \"distinct tile covering R\" for each j. It does not explicitly say that the tiles covering L are distinct from those covering R. However, the phrase \"distinct tile covering L\" means for each column j, there is a tile covering L that has rightmost column j. It doesn't forbid that tile also being the leftmost column for some column k. But if the tile's leftmost column is k, then that tile would also be the leftmost column for column k, which would satisfy the requirement for column k in the R argument. So the same tile could serve as both a rightmost tile for column j and leftmost tile for column k. That would reduce the total number of distinct tiles needed. The lower bound argument assumes that each column j must be rightmost column of a distinct tile, and each column j must be leftmost column of a distinct tile, but does not guarantee that those tiles are distinct across the two sets. So the lower bound might be too high. However, the construction uses exactly 2n-2 tiles, each tile either left of holes (rightmost column j) or right of holes (leftmost column j+1). In that construction, each tile is either left tile (rightmost column j) or right tile (leftmost column j+1). So each column j appears as rightmost column of left tile for column j (except j=n). And each column j appears as leftmost column of right tile for column j (except j=1). So each column appears twice (except extremes). So the construction uses 2n-2 tiles. Could we reduce the number by having some tiles that serve both roles? Let's think: Could we have a tile that is left of some holes and right of others, covering both left and right sides across rows? That tile would have leftmost column a and rightmost column b. For columns a..b, the tile covers those columns for certain rows. For each column j in [a,b], the tile could be leftmost column for column a, rightmost column for column b. But the lower bound requires each column j (except extremes) to be rightmost column of some tile covering L. That could be satisfied by tile with rightmost column j. Similarly each column j (except extremes) must be leftmost column of some tile covering R. That could be satisfied by tile with leftmost column j. So a tile could be rightmost column for j and leftmost column for k if its interval is [k,j] with k<j. So that tile would serve both roles for two columns. So we could potentially reduce count by having each tile cover multiple columns on both sides? But the tile's interval is contiguous; if it covers columns from k to j, then for any column between k and j, the tile is neither leftmost nor rightmost column; it's interior. So each tile can serve at most two columns: its leftmost column and its rightmost column. So each tile can cover at most two of the required column constraints (one for L, one for R). Since we need to satisfy 2(n-1) constraints (rightmost column for each j=1..n-1, leftmost column for each j=2..n), we need at least (2(n-1))/2 = n-1 tiles if each tile can satisfy two constraints. However, there is a catch: The constraints for L and R are for different sets of columns: L constraints are for j=1..n-1, R constraints are for j=2..n. So each tile could potentially satisfy one L constraint (its rightmost column) and one R constraint (its leftmost column). So in theory we could achieve n-1 tiles? But is that possible? Let's try to construct a tiling with n-1 tiles. Suppose we have a tile covering columns from 2 to n-1 (leftmost column 2, rightmost column n-1). This tile would satisfy L constraint for column n-1 (rightmost column) and R constraint for column 2 (leftmost column). But we also need L constraints for columns 1..n-2 and R constraints for columns 3..n. So we need many tiles. Perhaps we can pair constraints: For each j from 2 to n-1, we could have a tile with leftmost column j and rightmost column j (i.e., width 1). That would satisfy both L constraint for column j (rightmost column) and R constraint for column j (leftmost column). But a tile of width 1 cannot be both leftmost and rightmost column for the same column? Actually if tile covers only column j, then its leftmost and rightmost column are both j, so it would satisfy both constraints for column j (L: rightmost column j, R: leftmost column j). However, the L constraints are for j=1..n-1, and R constraints for j=2..n. So for columns 2..n-1, a tile covering just column j would satisfy both constraints for that column. For column 1, we need a tile with rightmost column 1 (L) but no R constraint for column 1. For column n, we need a tile with leftmost column n (R) but no L constraint for column n. So we could have n-1 tiles: one tile covering column 1 (rightmost column 1), one tile covering column n (leftmost column n), and for each j=2..n-1 a tile covering column j (both leftmost and rightmost). That would be n-1 + maybe 0? Actually that would be (n-2) + 2 = n tiles? Let's count: For j=2..n-1, that's n-2 columns, each tile covers that column. Then we need a tile covering column 1 (rightmost column 1) and a tile covering column n (leftmost column n). That's total (n-2)+2 = n tiles. So we would have n tiles. But the lower bound says at least 2n-2. So this suggests the lower bound may be too high. However, we must consider that each tile must be axis-aligned rectangle covering unit squares, and cannot cover any uncovered squares (holes). In the identity permutation case, holes are on diagonal. A tile covering column j only (i.e., width 1) would be a vertical strip covering all rows in column j. But that would include the hole at (j,j) which is uncovered. So a tile cannot cover column j entirely because it would cover the hole at (j,j). So vertical strips are not allowed. Similarly, any tile covering column j must avoid covering the hole at (j,j). So a tile covering column j can only cover rows i where π(i) ≠ j, i.e., rows where the hole is not in column j. Since holes are on diagonal, each column j has exactly one hole at row j. So a tile covering column j cannot include row j. So the tile could be a vertical strip covering all rows except row j. But a rectangular tile must be contiguous in rows as well. So to avoid row j, the tile could be split into two separate rectangles: one covering rows 1..j-1 in column j, and another covering rows j+1..n in column j. But those would be two separate tiles. So a single tile cannot cover column j without covering the hole unless it avoids that row, which would require a hole in the rectangle interior (since rectangle includes all rows between its top and bottom). So a rectangle covering column j must either be a horizontal rectangle that includes column j but not the row j? Actually a rectangle is defined by a set of rows [r1, r2] and columns [c1, c2]. If we want to avoid covering (j,j), we must ensure either j not in [r1,r2] or j not in [c1,c2]. So a rectangle covering column j (c1=c2=j) must have row interval that does not include row j. So it could be rows 1..j-1 (if j>1) or rows j+1..n (if j<n). That's allowed. So a vertical strip covering column j but only rows above the hole (or below) is a rectangle. However, that rectangle would be of height j-1 (or n-j). So it would be a tile covering column j but not the hole. That tile would be either left of the hole (if its rows are above the hole) or right of the hole? Actually left/right refers to column relative to hole, not row. So column j is the column of the hole. The tile covering column j but rows above the hole is still covering column j, which is the column of the hole. The tile's column interval includes column j, but the tile does not include the hole because the row interval excludes row j. So it's okay.\n\nNow, does the lower bound argument consider such tiles? Let's examine: For column j (1 ≤ j ≤ n-1), they consider the cell (i_j, j) where i_j is the row with hole at column j+1. That cell is left of the hole in row i_j. The tile covering that cell cannot extend to column j+1. So its rightmost column is j. However, the tile could have leftmost column less than j, and could have rows that include row i_j but also other rows. It could also include column j for rows where the hole is at column > j (i.e., rows where π(i) > j). For those rows, column j is left of the hole, so tile covering column j for those rows is okay. For rows where π(i) = j (i.e., row i=j), column j is the hole column; but tile cannot include row j because that would include the hole. So the tile must avoid row j. So the tile covering (i_j, j) may be a rectangle that includes column j and rows from some start to some end, but must exclude row j. That's possible.\n\nNow, for each column j, we need at least one tile whose rightmost column is j. That tile could also be leftmost column for some column k (maybe k=j). But if its leftmost column is also j, then the tile is just column j (width 1). But then it cannot include row j, so it must be a vertical strip that excludes row j, which is not possible as a rectangle because rectangle must be contiguous in rows. It could be rows 1..j-1 (if j>1) or rows j+1..n (if j<n). That is a rectangle of width 1 and height j-1 or n-j. That tile's leftmost column is also j. So it would satisfy both L and R constraints for column j? Wait, R constraint for column j is leftmost column = j. So yes, that tile would satisfy both constraints for column j (rightmost column = j and leftmost column = j). But does that tile also avoid covering the hole? It avoids row j, so it does not cover the hole at (j,j). So it's allowed. So we could have a tile that covers column j for rows not equal to j. That tile would be counted for both L and R constraints for column j. So the lower bound argument that each column j must be rightmost column of a distinct tile covering L and each column j must be leftmost column of a distinct tile covering R does not preclude the same tile being counted for both constraints for the same column j. However, the argument says \"each column j (1≤j≤n-1) is the rightmost column of a distinct tile covering L\" and \"each column j (2≤j≤n) is the leftmost column of a distinct tile covering R\". It does not assert that the tile covering L for column j is distinct from the tile covering R for column j. But the phrase \"distinct tile covering L\" might be interpreted as distinct across columns j, not across L vs R sets. So the lower bound could be overcounting if a tile can satisfy both constraints for a column. However, the construction used in the solution uses separate left and right tiles for each row, not such overlapping. So the lower bound might be not tight? Let's examine if we can achieve fewer than 2n-2 tiles. Let's try to construct a tiling with n tiles? Or maybe n+something? Let's think.\n\nWe need to cover all squares except the holes. The holes are a permutation matrix. The uncovered squares are exactly one per row and column. Tiles are axis-aligned rectangles covering cells, no overlap.\n\nGoal: minimize number of tiles. Equivalent to partition the board minus the holes into axis-aligned rectangles (tiles). This is a classic problem: given a permutation matrix, the complement can be partitioned into rectangles. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix is known to be 2n-2. Actually, I recall a known result: For an n×n board with a permutation of holes (one per row and column), the complement can be partitioned into at most 2n-2 rectangles, and this bound is tight. This is related to the concept of \"grid graph\" and \"rectangular decomposition\". The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix is exactly 2n-2. This is known as the \"rectangular covering number\" of a permutation matrix complement. I think the known result is that the minimal number of rectangles needed to cover all 1's in a permutation matrix (or zeros) is n, but for covering the complement it's 2n-2. Actually, there is a known problem: \"Given an n×n board with one missing square in each row and column, find the minimum number of rectangles needed to cover the remaining squares\". The answer is 2n-2. This is a known combinatorial geometry problem. So the solution's answer likely correct.\n\nThus the answer 4048 is correct.\n\nNow we need to evaluate the solution's reasoning: Are the methods valid? The solution uses combinatorial reasoning: defines L and R sets, uses argument about rightmost column of tile covering certain cells, deducing at least n-1 tiles for each side. Then constructs a tiling using identity permutation, with left and right horizontal tiles per row. This is a valid construction. The lower bound argument seems valid; we need to check if any hidden flaw: The argument that each column j must be the rightmost column of a distinct tile covering L. It uses the cell (i_j, j) which is the rightmost cell left of the hole in row i_j. The tile covering that cell cannot extend to column j+1 because that would include the hole. So its rightmost column is j. So each column j has a tile with rightmost column j. Could the same tile serve for multiple columns j? No, because a tile has a single rightmost column, so cannot be rightmost column for two distinct columns. So each column j requires a distinct tile. So we have at least n-1 distinct tiles for L. Similarly for R. However, could a tile be counted for both L and R constraints for different columns? Possibly, but the argument for R uses leftmost column of a tile covering a specific cell (i'_j, j). That tile's leftmost column is j. So a tile that has rightmost column j (for L) cannot have leftmost column j (unless width zero). So a tile cannot be counted for both L and R constraints for the same column, but could be counted for L constraint for column j and R constraint for column k (different columns). In that case, the tile would have rightmost column j and leftmost column k. Since k ≤ j (leftmost ≤ rightmost), this is possible. So the same tile could satisfy two constraints: one L for column j and one R for column k. So the lower bound of 2n-2 may not be minimal if we can pair constraints. However, the construction uses separate tiles for each L and R constraint, achieving 2n-2. The question is whether we can pair them to reduce count. Let's examine if we can pair them for all columns except extremes. For each tile, we could set leftmost column = k, rightmost column = j, covering columns k..j. This tile would satisfy L constraint for column j and R constraint for column k. So each tile could satisfy two constraints. There are 2(n-1) constraints total (n-1 L constraints + n-1 R constraints). If each tile can satisfy at most two constraints, we need at least n-1 tiles. So lower bound could be n-1. But is it possible to satisfy all constraints with n-1 tiles? Let's try to construct such a tiling.\n\nWe need to cover all cells left of holes (L) and right of holes (R). The constraints are: for each column j (1..n-1), there must be a tile whose rightmost column is j and that tile covers some cel